from ss import *
L(6,'social-science','the-value-of-work','The Value of Work',
 ('Geeta Aunty and her neighbours','Anu and Kabir were playing in the park when they saw Geeta Aunty step out of a taxi. She is a pilot in the Indian Air Force, the pride of the town. Another neighbour, a retired Border Security Force man, now grows vegetables and teaches computer skills to young people on weekends. Everyone around us does so many things every day. Which of these are work for pay, and which are done out of love or duty? In this chapter we learn the value of all kinds of work.'),
 [('Economic and non-economic activities',['Activities that people do can be divided into two groups: economic activities and non-economic activities.','Economic activities are those done to earn money or to produce goods and services that are exchanged, such as a pilot flying for a salary, a farmer selling vegetables or a teacher paid a monthly salary.','Non-economic activities are done without expecting money: parents cooking for the family, children helping with homework, youth caring for grandparents, neighbours helping to repair a house or a volunteer teaching without a fee.','Some people do both. Geeta Aunty serves the country and gets a regular monthly salary, which is an economic activity. A retired soldier who also volunteers teaches for free, which is a non-economic activity.']),
  ('Markets, goods and services',['A market is a place where people exchange goods and services. Goods are things we can touch, such as rice and chairs. Services are help provided by people, such as a doctor\'s treatment or a driver\'s trip.','People may exchange goods and services for other goods and services, but in most cases they use money.']),
  ('How people are paid',['A salary is a regular payment, usually monthly, from an employer. A fee is paid for professional advice or services, as to a doctor or lawyer.','Payment in cash is in money. Payment in kind is a non-cash payment: in Kavya\'s aunt\'s village a worker on the orchard gets part of the payment in cash and the rest in mangoes of equal value.','Wages are paid per day or per task, such as to a construction worker operating an excavator on the new highway.']),
  ('Adding value',['Economic activities add value at each stage as something is changed into another form. A carpenter buys wood for Rs 600 and makes a chair that sells for Rs 1,000. The extra Rs 400 pays for his skill, time and effort. He has added value.','Roads, hospitals, schools and shops all add value to our lives.']),
  ('The value of non-economic work',['Non-economic activities do not earn money but have great value. Langars or community kitchens at gurudwaras feed every visitor free, and temples distribute prasad.','Community participation is another strong value. The Swachh Bharat Abhiyan depends on the efforts of all citizens to keep surroundings clean, and Van Mahotsav, the festival of forests, spreads awareness about trees.','Such work builds bonds, saves resources and creates the caring society in which economic activity can flourish. All honest work deserves dignity.'])],
 [('Classify each activity as economic or non-economic: a tailor stitching clothes for pay; a child watering plants at home; a bus driver; a neighbour who helps an elder cross the road.',[('A tailor stitching for pay is economic.','Done to earn money.'),('Watering plants at home and helping an elder are non-economic.','Done without payment.'),('A bus driver earns wages, so it is economic.','Paid work.')]),
  ('A farmer buys seeds for Rs 2,000, grows vegetables and sells them for Rs 5,500. How much value did the farm work add?',[('Selling price minus buying price: 5,500 minus 2,000.','Value added is the difference.'),('5,500 - 2,000 = 3,500.','Compute.'),('So Rs 3,500 of value was added by the work and effort.','State the result.')])],
 ('Why are non-economic activities valuable even though they bring no money?',['They help family and community.','They build values like care and cooperation.','Examples: langar, Swachh Bharat Abhiyan.'],['Thinking only paid work has value.','Confusing goods with services.']),
 ['Work is of two kinds: economic and non-economic.','People are paid by salary, fee, wages, in cash or in kind.','Work adds value to materials and to life.','Non-economic work and community participation have great value.'],
 [('m','An economic activity is one done','to earn money or produce things for exchange','only for fun','only on holidays','It involves earning or exchange.'),
  ('m','A teacher paid a monthly salary is doing','an economic activity','a non-economic activity','no activity','Salary shows it is economic.'),
  ('m','Which is a non-economic activity?','A grandchild caring for grandparents','A shopkeeper selling goods','A pilot flying for pay','It is done without payment.'),
  ('t','A langar at a gurudwara is an example of a non-economic activity.',True,'Food is served free to all.'),
  ('m','Payment in kind means','payment in goods instead of cash','payment in a cheque','payment in coins only','The worker got mangoes of equal value.'),
  ('m','A carpenter buys wood for Rs 600 and sells a chair for Rs 1000. The value added is','Rs 400','Rs 1600','Rs 600','1000 minus 600 is 400.'),
  ('m','A market is a place where','goods and services are exchanged','only cars are repaired','no one meets','Buyers and sellers meet there.'),
  ('t','Only paid work has value in society.',False,'Non-economic work also has great value.'),
  ('m','Swachh Bharat Abhiyan depends on','efforts of all citizens','only a few officers','foreign help','It aims to keep surroundings clean.'),
  ('m','Services include','a doctor\'s treatment','a bag of rice','a chair','Services are help, not things.')],
 keep=['economic','salary','value'])
